## the system
The Playdate is a small handheld with a monochrome display, limited resolution, and fairly constrained CPU and memory bandwidth for this kind of software rendering work. It is not a machine where one can casually throw around a full modern 3D pipeline.

That is exactly what makes it interesting. The hardware is capable enough to allow ambitious ideas, but limited enough that performance, memory access, and representation all matter. I like that alot and the 'unlimited CPU' and 'lua' is, for example, what kept me away from the Pico-8.

## why a voxel engine
Voxel landscapes are a very appealing fit for a machine like this. They can produce large-scale outdoor scenes, strong depth, and a distinctive visual style without requiring conventional polygon rendering.

What interests me here is not just the renderer itself, but the full chain around it: terrain data, world structure, visibility, explosions, depth handling, islands, water, and eventually gameplay.

The aim is not a pure tech demo. I want a real world with atmosphere and tension, probably closer to lonely exploration, stealth, and collapsing technical ruins than to a straightforward combat game.
We have more than enough war going on already.

## what is interesting technically
The Playdate project is interesting to me because it sits between old and new constraints. The machine is much more capable than the 8-bit systems I often work on, but performance still depends heavily on making careful choices.

Topics that matter here include:
* cache-friendly terrain layout
* chunked world data for larger maps
* direct LCD rendering
* depth buffering for terrain and effects
* explosion rendering and occlusion
* terrain generation from real-world data
* extracting more visual richness from a 1-bit screen

A recurring theme is that RAM access matters a great deal. Some optimizations are less about reducing arithmetic and more about arranging data so the machine can reach it efficiently.

## project status
Right now the project has moved beyond the earliest experiment stage:
* a working voxel landscape renderer
* direct screen rendering
* ongoing work on depth handling
* experiments with explosions and screen-space effects
* terrain-generation workflow for island landscapes
* continuing work on performance and cache behavior

The next steps are to keep improving depth-aware effects, refine terrain generation, and move the whole thing further from "interesting renderer" toward an actual game world.

